#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "The semantic layer"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "The semantic layer is where every measure and indicator is defined, once, in version control. Dashboards, natural-language query, agents, published statistics and international returns all read from it. So an enrolment ratio means the same thing on every screen, in every file and in every release ([P11]{1}, [CAP-SEMANTICS]{2})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "This matters more for an EMIS than for most platforms. People compare official indicators across years, areas and countries. If a definition drifts between two tools, you end up with two \"official\" figures, and only one of them can be right."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "How it works"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "Why it is drawn this way"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "The definitions sit on top of the counts in gold. Gold stores counts that can be added up, and the semantic layer computes every ratio from them, so you get the right answer at any level of aggregation ([CAP-TRANSFORMATION.5]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "Everything a consumer reads is generated. CI produces the engine-native metric objects and the saved-query tables from the definitions, and nobody writes them by hand ([CAP-SEMANTICS.8]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "A parity test closes the loop. Every serving path must return the same numbers for every saved query, and that's what \"defined once\" means in practice ([CAP-SEMANTICS.9]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "Published statistics come from saved-query tables, stamped with the definition's version and the reference date, never from a live query."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "Rules that keep one definition portable"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "Model each indicator as a ratio of counts that add up, on one gold table. The numerator and the denominator are counts at the same grain, meaning the same reference date, area, sex, age, level and so on. If the denominator comes from outside, such as a population estimate, join it into gold along with its version. That way every engine computes the ratio the same way."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "Stick to a portable subset of metric types. That means simple counts and sums, ratios, and derived expressions without time offsets. Anything else, such as cumulative windows, offsets or conversion funnels, needs a recorded and tested mapping for each serving engine ([CAP-SEMANTICS.7]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "Keep each indicator's metadata with its definition, and check it in CI. The metadata gives the code, the edition of the international definition it follows, the unit, the reference-date rule, the disaggregations allowed, the coverage rule, the owner and the version ([CAP-SEMANTICS.3]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "Version the definitions together with the models that produce their counts, in the same project and in the same review ([CAP-SEMANTICS.6]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "In the reference implementations"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "Both reference implementations use the **dbt semantic layer** ([ADR-0011]{1}). You write semantic models and metrics in dbt's YAML specification (the latest one, for dbt 1.12 and later), and **MetricFlow**, which is licensed under Apache-2.0, validates and compiles them."
msgstr ""

